'''Ross 154''' ('''V1216 Sagittarii''') is a [[red dwarf]] [[star]] in the southern [[zodiac]] [[constellation]] of [[Sagittarius (constellation)|Sagittarius]]. It has an [[apparent visual magnitude]] of 10.44,<ref name=RECONS/> making it much too faint to be seen with the [[naked eye]]. At a minimum, viewing Ross 154 requires a telescope with an [[aperture]] of {{Convert|6.5|cm|in|sigfig=1|abbr=on}} under ideal conditions.<ref name=mills1994/> The distance to this star can be estimated from [[parallax]] measurements, which places it at {{Convert|9.71|ly|pc|abbr=off|lk=on}} away from [[Earth]].<ref name="Gaia3"/> It is the nearest star in the southern constellation [[Sagittarius (constellation)|Sagittarius]], and one of the [[List of nearest stars and brown dwarfs|nearest stars]] to the Sun.<ref name=Kirkpatrick2024/>

==Description==
This star was first catalogued by American astronomer [[Frank Elmore Ross]] in 1925, and formed part of his fourth list of new variable stars.<ref name=aj37_91/> In 1926, he added it to his second list of stars showing a measurable [[proper motion]] after comparing its position with photographic plates taken earlier by fellow American astronomer [[Edward Emerson Barnard|E. E. Barnard]].<ref name=aj36_856_124/> A preliminary [[parallax]] value of {{val|0.362|0.006|ul=arcseconds}} was determined in 1937 by Walter O'Connell using photographic plates from the Yale telescope in [[Johannesburg]], [[South Africa]]. This placed the star at the sixth position of the then-known nearby stars.<ref name=aj46_1078_204/>

[[File:V1216SgrLightCurve.png|thumb|left|A broadband optical [[light curve]] for V1216 Sagittarii, plotted from ''[[Kepler space telescope|Kepler]]'' data<ref name=MAST/>]]
Ross 154 was found to be a [[UV Ceti]]-type [[flare star]], with a mean time between major flares of about two&nbsp;days.<ref name=ibvs1221_1/> The first such flare activity was observed from Australia in 1951 when the star increased in magnitude by 0.4.<ref name=jrasc47_23/> Typically, the star will increase by 3–4 magnitudes during a flare.<ref name=ibvs461_1/> The strength of the star's surface [[Stellar magnetic field|magnetic field]] is an estimated {{val|2.2|0.1|ul=kG}}.<ref name=apj656_2_1121/> Ross 154 is an [[X-ray astronomy|X-ray]] source and it has been detected by several X-ray observatories. The quiescent X-ray luminosity is about {{val|9|e=27|u=ergs/s}}.<ref name=apj676/> X-ray flare emission from this star has been observed by [[Chandra X-ray Observatory|Chandra observatory]], with a particularly large flare emitting {{val|2.3|e=33|u=erg}}.<ref name=apj676/>

A [[stellar classification]] of M3.5V<ref name=RECONS/> makes this a [[red dwarf]] star that is generating energy through the [[nuclear fusion]] of hydrogen at its core. It has an estimated 18% of the [[solar mass|Sun's mass]] and 20% of the [[solar radius|Sun's radius]],<ref name=Pineda2021/> but it is radiating only 0.4% of the [[solar luminosity|luminosity of the Sun]].<ref name=Pineda2021/> In contrast to the Sun where convection only occurs in the outer layers, a red dwarf with a mass this low will be entirely [[convection|convective]].<ref name=aaa496_3_787/> Based on the relatively high [[stellar rotation|projected rotation]], this is probably a young star with an estimated age of less than a billion years.<ref name=apj676/> The abundance of elements heavier than helium is about half that in the Sun.<ref name=apj676/>

The [[Space velocity (astronomy)|space velocity]] components of this star in the [[galactic coordinate system]] are {{nowrap|[''U'', ''V'', ''W'']}} = [–12.2, –1.0, –7.2]{{nowrap| km s<sup>−1</sup>}}.<ref name=cdsannot/> It has not been identified as a member of a specific stellar [[moving group]]<ref name=mnras328_1_45/> and is orbiting through the [[Milky Way]] galaxy at a distance from the core that varies from {{Convert|27.65|-|30.66|kly|kpc|abbr=on|lk=on}} with an [[orbital eccentricity]] of 0.052.<ref name=rmaa34_37/> Based on its low velocity relative to the Sun, this is believed to be a young disk ([[population I]]) star, though it has a lower metallicity than the Sun.<ref name=aj79_702/> This star will make its closest approach to the Sun in about 157,000&nbsp;years, when it comes within {{Convert|6.39|+/-|0.10|ly|pc|abbr=on}}.<ref name=al36_3_220/>

==Search for planets==
No [[brown dwarf]] or gas giant companions have been discovered in orbit around Ross 154.<ref name=aj123_4_2027/> Nor does it display the level of [[infrared excess|excess infrared emission]] that would suggest the presence of circumstellar dust. Such [[debris disk]]s are rare among M-type star systems older than about 10 million years, having been primarily cleared away by drag from the [[stellar wind]].<ref name=apj631_2_1161/>

Ross 154 was observed with the [[HARPS]] spectrograph in 2017 as part of the [[Red Dots campaign]], and has also been observed with the [[CARMENES]] spectrograph. These observations show [[radial velocity]] variations with a 7-day period, which could be due to either an orbiting [[super-Earth]] planet ([[minimum mass]] {{Earth mass|1.5~2|link=y}}) or intrinsic [[stellar activity]]. Further observations are needed to confirm or refute the presence of a planet.<ref name=Brown2026/>

